// `min-width 0` on every link in this chain, not just the leaf. A flex item
// defaults to `min-width auto`, which refuses to shrink below its content, so a
// shortfall becomes OVERFLOW of the ancestor rather than compression here. The
// leaf already carried it and that was not enough: measured in the 70px bar
// back when this group still carried four columns, `.auction__bid-info` ran
// 487px of content in a 407px box and the details group's right edge sat 79px
// past where `.auction__bid-actions` begins, so `BYE` and `AGE` rendered
// underneath the bid controls. The two intermediate boxes were the ones
// refusing to shrink; the leaf could not compress while its parents would not.
//
// A HORIZONTAL CLIP, NOT `overflow hidden`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE
// REASON THIS RULE IS ODD-LOOKING.
//
// What has to be clipped is the NAME, sideways: `min-width 0` lets this box
// shrink, but without a clip the text inside still paints at full width over
// whatever follows, and the symptom is identical to not having shrunk at all --
// measured at a 1280px viewport, this box was 224px wide holding 264px of name,
// printing "PICKETT" straight through "MARKET $0". A box-overlap check cannot
// see it either: the boxes tile perfectly while the ink does not.
//
// What must NOT be clipped is the headshot, upward: from 1160px it is 131px
// tall in a 70px bar and rises out of the chrome on purpose. It is a sibling of
// the name inside this box, so `overflow hidden` cannot tell the two apart --
// it cut the large headshot off flat at the bar's top edge.
//
// `clip-path` separates the axes, which `overflow` cannot: setting one axis of
// `overflow` to hidden forces the other from `visible` to `auto`, so
// `overflow-x hidden; overflow-y visible` is not a thing that exists. The inset
// takes the right, bottom and left edges at the border box and opens the top
// edge far enough that nothing will reach it.
//
// THE CLIP HAS TO LIVE AT THIS LEVEL and cannot move down onto the name block.
// player-name.styl gives `.player__name-lead` `flex 0 0 auto`, so it is sized
// to its content and never smaller -- and a box that is never smaller than its
// content has nothing to clip. Making it shrinkable so it could clip itself is
// what an earlier draft of this did, and it inverted the shrink order: the
// details group is `flex 1 1 0%`, a basis of ZERO, so all of the shortfall came
// out of the name instead. Measured that way at 1440px, the widest desktop
// here, the name block was 96px and "ADAM PRENTICE" rendered as "ADAM PREN".
// The name is the subject of this surface; it takes the width it needs and the
// details divide the rest.
.nominated__player
  display flex
  flex 1
  min-width 0
  clip-path inset(-999px 0 0 0)

  .player__headshot
    border-radius 0

// THE RIGHT GUTTER IS UNCONDITIONAL, because what it separates this group from
// is different either side of 1160 and it is needed in both. Stacked, the bar
// runs edge to edge and `Live value` ended against the SCREEN. On the single
// desktop row it ends against `.auction__bid-actions`, and the two boxes were
// measured touching at exactly 0px of gap at 1160, 1280, 1440, 1600 and 1920.
//
// The left half of the gutter is not: it is 8px in the stacked zone only, where
// `.player__name-headshot` carries a matching 8px margin and the two add up to
// the 16 that `.auction__text` takes. On the desktop row the headshot has no
// such margin and this group starts at the bar's own centred edge, which needs
// nothing.
.auction__nominated-player
  flex 1
  display flex
  min-width 0
  padding-right 16px
  // Each detail clips its OWN ink. The group above having `overflow hidden` is
  // not enough and the distinction is easy to get wrong: that one stops the
  // group painting outside itself, while this stops each item painting over the
  // item beside it. Measured at 900px with the four columns this group used to
  // carry, the labels wanted about 170px inside a 132px group -- wrapped, which
  // is what they do at that width -- and without this the shortfall came out as
  // MARKET printed through LIVE VALUE rather than as either one being cut off.
  // Two columns is more room, and below 1160 the bar now stacks and hands this
  // group the full width, so the squeeze that motivated the rule is gone -- but
  // the rule stays, because it is what stops the two columns painting over each
  // other at the narrow end where the bar is 400px wide.
  // `flex 0 1 auto` for the same reason the group above takes it: a basis of 0
  // makes each column claim an equal share of whatever the group was given
  // rather than the width its own label and value need, so `Market` and
  // `Live value` would divide the group between them however little either had
  // to say. Content-sized, the group's own width means something, which is what
  // lets it hand the surplus to the name.
  .selected__player-header-item
    min-width 0
    flex 0 1 auto
    overflow hidden

// `overflow hidden` as well as `min-width 0`, the same pairing argued for at
// the top of this file and for the same reason: `min-width 0` lets the BOX
// shrink while the text inside keeps painting at full width over whatever
// follows. This box had only the first half, and a nowrap label was all it took
// to expose it -- at a 900px viewport the labels printed through each other and
// through the headshot ("ARKETVA...", "$0$0") while every box still tiled
// perfectly, so a box-overlap check read clean.
// `flex 0 1 auto`, NOT `flex 1`, AND DROPPING COLUMNS DOES NOTHING WITHOUT IT.
// `flex 1` is `flex 1 1 0%`, which GROWS to an equal share of the player half
// whatever it is holding -- so removing `Bye` and `Age` freed exactly nothing
// the first time: this group kept all 284px of its 1440px share and the two
// survivors simply spread out over it, while the name next door still had 104px
// for the 178px it needs. Measured before and after the columns came out,
// `details_box` was 284px both times.
//
// Sized to content instead, this group takes what its two columns actually
// need and `.nominated__player` -- which is still `flex 1` -- absorbs the whole
// remainder, which is where the name lives. Shrink stays at 1 so the squeeze
// zone below 1160px compresses the details rather than pushing the bar wide.
.nominated__player-details
  flex 0 1 auto
  display flex
  min-width 0
  overflow hidden
  padding-top 8px
  font-size 12px
  label
    font-size 10px
    font-family "Inter", "Helvetica", "Arial", sans-serif;
    font-weight 300
    text-transform uppercase
    display block
    padding-bottom 6px

// `MARKET VALUE` IS TWO WORDS AND MUST NOT BREAK BETWEEN THEM, because the
// label is what sets this box's width and the price inside it is centred on
// that width. Wrapped, the box narrows to `MARKET` and the centring follows it,
// which reads as the number having moved rather than as the label having
// wrapped.
//
// UNCONDITIONAL, where the old two-word label's nowrap was floored at 1024px.
// That floor was drawn when this group carried four columns into a squeeze
// zone; it carries ONE now, so the group is about 75px wide at every width and
// there is nothing left for a wrap to relieve.
.nominated__player-details label
  white-space nowrap

// THE VALUE IS IN A BOX because it is now the only price on this surface and it
// is a live one: recomputed from the cap money still unspent every time a
// player is sold, so it moves under the reader while a preseason figure would
// not. The box says "this is the number this bar is about" against a bar that
// also carries a bid, a timer and a set of controls.
//
// White on a rule, against the bar's own $backgroundColor ground, so the
// distinction survives without spending a colour: the bar already carries the
// election amount in $accentColor a few inches to the right, and a second
// accented number here would read as though the two were related. The inflation
// delta inside the box is the one exception and it earns it -- see below.
//
// `flex 0 0 auto` rather than a `flex 1 1 0` equal share. The padding and border
// have to come out of somewhere, and taking it from a share of the group would
// squeeze the contents into the box they just gained; sized to content the box
// takes what it needs and the surplus goes back to `.nominated__player`, which
// is where the name lives.
// QUALIFIED BY THE PARENT ON PURPOSE, and it is not style. `padding` here
// competes with `.selected__player-header-item` in selected-player.styl, which
// sets `padding 4px 12px` and which this element also carries -- both selectors
// rank 0,1,0, so which one wins is decided by chunk order rather than by intent.
// It won: an earlier draft of this rule declared its own padding, emitted it,
// and never applied it, which is this repo's characteristic CSS failure and is
// invisible from the stylesheet. The parent qualifier lifts this to 0,2,0 so it
// cannot be quietly outranked.
//
// `align-self center` rather than the default `stretch`. An item with no
// visible box did not care that it was stretched; this one did -- boxed, it ran
// the full height of the details group, leaving 6px above its rule and 20px
// below.
.nominated__player-details .nominated__detail-value
  flex 0 0 auto
  align-self center
  padding 4px 10px
  background white
  border 1px solid $borderColor
  border-radius $button_border_radius
  font-weight 600

// ONLY THE BOX IS HERE. How the price and its inflation delta relate to each
// other is the component's own business and lives in player-auction-value.styl,
// which the selected-player drawer shares -- this file owns the treatment that
// makes the item read as part of THIS bar and nothing more.

// IN FLOW, NOT FLOATED. This was a 100px circle at `position absolute;
// top -96px`, which put 96 of its 100 pixels OUTSIDE the bar -- and the bar is
// the last thing on the page, so what sits above it is page content. Measured
// at 768: it covered 13% of the standing elections row and overlapped the live
// blocks header; at 600, 17%. The float was never the design, it was a
// workaround for a bar that had no room -- `.auction__bid-info` declared 105px
// inside a 120px bar that also owed 46px to the controls, so flex-shrink
// crushed the player half to 74px and a 100px circle could not fit in it. That
// declared 105px was a fiction (the contents measure 69px) and removing it in
// auction-main-bid.styl is what buys the room for this rule to exist.
//
// 64px, and the height comes from the text block either way. The name and the
// details row stack to 69px, so anything up to that is free: measured, the
// player half is 69px tall with a 64px headshot, with a 56px one, and with no
// headshot at all. Sized larger it would start driving the bar's height and
// buy back the original problem.
//
// 1159, WHICH IS WHERE THE BAR STOPS BEING A SINGLE ROW. This window is for the
// stacked bar, and the stacked bar is every width below the cap -- not just the
// phone. A picture bigger than this does not overflow a stacked bar, it makes it
// taller: measured at 1024 with the large size, the bar went to 177px against a
// 120px reservation. `getHeadshotWidth()` in auction-nominated-player.js tests
// the same boundary and must move with it.
@media (max-width 1159px)
  .auction__nominated-player .player__name-headshot
    overflow hidden
    flex 0 0 auto
    align-self center
    margin-left 8px
    background rgba(255,255,255,0.8)
    border 1px solid $borderColor
    border-radius 50%
    width 64px
    height 64px
    display flex
    align-items flex-end
    justify-content center

// THE PICTURE'S OWN SIZE IS NOT SET HERE and cannot be. PlayerHeadshot writes
// width and height onto the avatar as an inline style, which no rule in this
// file can outrank -- so the image is sized by NARROW_HEADSHOT_WIDTH in
// auction-nominated-player.js, and the 88x64 it renders is what fills the 64px
// window above. Change one and the other has to move with it.

@media (max-width 500px)
  .auction__nominated-player .selected__player-header-item
    padding 4px
    flex 0 0 auto
    min-width 24px

// THE DETAILS DROP BELOW THE NAME rather than sharing its line with it. The name
// half needs about 250px -- a 64px headshot, its 8px margin, and 178px of "ADAM
// PRENTICE" -- and MARKET with LIVE VALUE need about 131px, so the row stops
// fitting below roughly 381px. The name is what gives when it does not fit:
// `.player__name-lead` is `flex 0 0 auto` and cannot shrink, so the shortfall
// lands on `.nominated__player` and the clip-path at the top of this file cuts
// the name off. Measured, it rendered "PRENTI" at 320 and "PRENT" at 300, with
// every box still tiling perfectly -- the ink is clipped, not the layout, which
// is why an overlap check reads clean through it.
//
// A ROW THAT CANNOT FIT IS THE SAME PROBLEM THE ACTIONS ROW HAS one box down,
// and it takes the same answer. The name is the subject of this surface;
// clipping it to keep a second reading of a $0 market value on the same line is
// the wrong trade at any width.
//
// 400 rather than 500: at 420 the row measures clean and the name renders in
// full, so the two boxes still belong on one line there. 400 is the boundary
// `.auction__main-timer` already uses in auction-main-bid.styl.
//
// THE MEASUREMENTS ABOVE AND BELOW PREDATE THE MERGE of `Market` and `Live
// value` into one `Value` column, so the details group is narrower than the
// ~131px and ~115.9px they quote. They are kept as the derivation of the
// breakpoint rather than as a current reading: one column is strictly less than
// two, so every fit they argue for still fits. Re-measure before moving 400.
@media (max-width 400px)
  .auction__nominated-player
    flex-wrap wrap
    row-gap 2px

  // `flex 1 1 auto`, so this box's BASE SIZE is its content rather than the
  // `flex 1` basis of ZERO it takes above. A zero-basis item can never force a
  // wrap -- it reports no content to the line-breaking pass, so the details
  // would stay beside it however little room was left. Confined to this query
  // because above it that same zero basis is what makes the details shrink
  // before the name does.
  .auction__nominated-player .nominated__player
    flex 1 1 auto

  // The 8px was the offset that put this group's baseline on the name's while
  // the two shared a line. On its own line it is what leaves a gap under the
  // name instead.
  .auction__nominated-player .nominated__player-details
    padding-top 0

  // THE NAME BLOCK COMES DOWN SO THE ROW FITS ONE LINE AT 320, and the budget is
  // arithmetic rather than a tuned number. The row has 296px there -- 320 less
  // the 8 and 16 of gutter -- and it was spending 370.2: a 76px headshot with its
  // margins, a 178.3px name block, and 115.9px of Market and Live value. 74.2px
  // over.
  //
  // 40 OF THAT 178.3 WAS PADDING, so that is where it comes from first. The
  // block is `padding 8px 16px 0 24px` in player-name.styl -- an asymmetric
  // gutter for a name sitting beside a headshot in a wide table row, which is
  // not what this is. 12px here, and the type carries the separation instead.
  //
  // The rest comes off the last name, 2em to 1.25em: 28px to 17.5px, which takes
  // ADAM PRENTICE from 138.3px of ink to 86.4. It stays the largest thing in the
  // block -- the first name above it is 14px -- so the hierarchy that makes the
  // surname the subject survives the reduction.
  //
  // Together: 72 + 98.4 + 115.9 against 296, with room to spare rather than a
  // hairline fit. THE SPARE IS THE POINT -- ADAM PRENTICE is eight characters and
  // this league has names twice that, so a reduction tuned to fit exactly this
  // one would wrap again for the next nomination.
  .auction__nominated-player .player__name-lead
    // `flex 0 1 auto` and `min-width 0`, replacing the `flex 0 0 auto` that
    // makes this block content-sized and unshrinkable everywhere else. It is the
    // guard for the long name the numbers above cannot pre-empt: shrink first,
    // and the ellipsis below rather than pushing the row wider. Safe HERE and not
    // above, because the details beside it are `flex 0 1 auto` too now -- when
    // they were `flex 1 1 0%` a shrinkable name took the whole shortfall and
    // rendered ADAM PREN at 1440.
    flex 0 1 auto
    min-width 0
    padding 8px 4px 0 8px

  .auction__nominated-player .player__name-last
    font-size 1.25em

  // ELLIPSIS, NOT A CLIP. `.nominated__player` clips with a clip-path so the
  // headshot can rise out of the bar, and a name that meets that edge is simply
  // cut mid-letter. These two shorten with a marker instead, which is what
  // `.player__name-top` already does one component over.
  .auction__nominated-player .player__name-first
  .auction__nominated-player .player__name-last
    white-space nowrap
    overflow hidden
    text-overflow ellipsis
